=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router7
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The physical/L2 interface `eth3` on `router7`, which carries the `link_wan_10_0_0` WAN segment (10.0.0.0/24) connecting router7 to router10.

**Mechanism:** `router7.eth3` is in `operstate DOWN` (Linux flags: `<BROADCAST,MULTICAST>` — no `UP`, no `LOWER_UP`). Because the interface is down, OSPF cannot form or maintain an adjacency on the 10.0.0.0/24 segment. From router10's perspective, its `eth2` (10.0.0.2/24) remains declared in OSPF but shows `nbr=0, adj=0` — it is a DR with no neighbor, confirming the link is broken at both ends. Traffic between Campus 1 and Campus 2 is rerouted via longer alternative paths (router7 → router1 → router11 → router17, or router7 → router8 → router17), producing the observed higher latency with no full outage.

**Localization:** `router7`, interface `eth3`. The startup log confirms `ip link set eth3 up` was executed at boot, so the interface was intentionally UP at initialization. `carrier_changes=2` (minimal — one up at boot, one down transition) and no flap agent in `ps` confirm this is a static `link_down`, not a flap. All other router7 interfaces are UP and OSPF-healthy.

**Root cause:** `router7.eth3` has been brought down after startup (fault-injected `link_down`), silencing the OSPF adjacency to router10 and forcing inter-campus traffic onto suboptimal paths.

**Fix:** Execute `ip link set eth3 up` on router7. This restores carrier on the 10.0.0.0/24 segment, allowing OSPF to re-establish the Full adjacency with router10 and resume optimal routing. No ospfd restart is needed — the daemon is healthy and has never crashed.
=== END ===